WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
Instruction tuning.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:06.257
This is the one step that turns a text completer into an assistant.

3
00:00:06.257 --> 00:00:08.600
And the dataset is small enough to read.

4
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.767
Look at what happens before and after instruction tuning.

5
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.033
We ask: what causes solar flares?

6
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:18.633
A base model continues the document, like a quiz page.

7
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.200
After tuning, the model replies with a helpful answer.

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.767
Instruction tuning is fine-tuning on pairs: an instruction, and the response we want.

9
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:34.267
The model learns a new prior: instruction-shaped text is followed by a helpful answer.

10
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:41.700
Famous datasets like Alpaca, with fifty two thousand pairs, and Dolly, with fifteen thousand, are small and public.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.133
The mechanism is surprisingly plain.

12
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:49.067
Each example is just JSON with an instruction and an output.

13
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.567
A template wraps them with marker words.

14
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:53.400
That gives one training string.

15
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.074
And we fine-tune on it.

16
00:00:55.074 --> 00:00:58.600
Those markers are the ancestors of every chat format today.

17
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.000
Here it is in code.

18
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.737
We load Alpaca and look at one example.

19
00:01:03.737 --> 00:01:06.300
It has just an instruction and an output.

20
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.075
Then we glue them into one string, with an instruction marker and a response marker.

21
00:01:11.075 --> 00:01:12.300
That's the whole trick.

22
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.933
Try it for your own domain.

23
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:20.100
Write five pairs: a telemetry excerpt in, a perfect anomaly report out.

24
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.033
Use exactly the format your system should produce.

25
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:28.900
These pairs seed your later SFT dataset, and they teach you why data quality dominates.

26
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:30.840
Let's recap.

27
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.033
Instruction tuning is fine-tuning on instruction and response pairs.

28
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:37.433
It turns a completer into an assistant.

29
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:40.200
The data is just JSON plus a template.

30
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.875
And the quality of the pairs matters most.

31
00:01:43.875 --> 00:01:45.000
Next up: RLHF.
